Requirements
Requirement	Module	Access program	Statement
data-bank.1	data bank		The data bank MUST store the files the converter writes for each of its inputs, and communicate them to another system.
data-bank.3	data bank		The data bank MUST give the access programs of storage and communication as its own: folder, insert and stored of storage, and names, records and new of communication.
converter.1	converter	convert	The converter MUST read an input of JSON values that a module specification describes, and write the whole input as one MTSV file.
converter.5	converter	sheets	The access program sheets MUST give _sheets and _fields of the input named: every sheet the schema gives, by its place in the order of the schema's tree, and the name of each field of its header.
converter-input.1	converter › input	read	The converter's input MUST be read in full, the module specification first and then each input value, before any value is transferred.
converter-input.2	converter › input	read	The input MUST give the schema's instances and runs, the input values' _instances and _runs, each value's instances keyed by its position in the whole input, and annotations, as schema validates each input value.
schema.1	schema	schema	A module specification MUST supply a schema of draft-07, valid against its metaschema, as a JSON text of any size, any depth of nesting and any length of string, against which every value of its input validates.
schema.2	schema	validate	The access program validate MUST give, for each instance of each input value, each subschema applied to it and whether the instance is valid against it, and reject an input value that fails an assertion as non-conforming, its reason the value's position, a space, and the JSON Pointer to the deepest instance location where it fails, in its URI fragment representation.
schema.3	schema	schema	The root schema MUST hold a title annotation, which names the sheet of the input values.
schema.4	schema	schema	Every name the schema gives a sheet or a column, a title, a property's name, a key of patternProperties or dependencies, or the last reference token of a $ref, MUST be text a field can hold, holding no HT, LF, FF or CR.
schema.5	schema	schema	A regular expression of pattern or patternProperties MUST use only the tokens schema authors should limit themselves to: individual characters, simple and complemented character classes and ranges, the quantifiers +, *, ?, {x}, {x,y} and {x,} and their lazy forms, the anchors ^ and $, and simple grouping and alternation, none taken to be anchored.
schema.17	schema	schema	The access program schema MUST give each $ref of the schema with the location of the schema it references, its fragment percent-decoded as UTF-8 and evaluated as a JSON Pointer.
schema.6	schema	schema	A $ref MUST reference a schema within the supplied schema, the one schema the converter is given.
schema.7	schema	schema	A $ref's fragment MUST be a JSON Pointer in its URI fragment representation, of the characters a URI fragment holds, $id not being read.
schema.8	schema	schema	A $ref MUST NOT reach again, at the same instance location through allOf, anyOf, oneOf, not, if, then, else or a dependency, a schema it is applied from.
schema.9	schema	schema	A module specification that breaks schema.1 or schema.3-8 MUST be rejected as non-conforming.
schema.11	schema	validate	A $ref MUST be applied as the schema it references, as schema gives it, its other members ignored.
schema.13	schema	validate	Every assertion of the schema MUST be applied in validation.
schema.16	schema	validate	A keyword that is neither a validation keyword nor $ref MUST be ignored in validation, asserting nothing: format, contentMediaType and contentEncoding among them.
value.1	value	values	The input values, each a JSON text in UTF-8, MUST be read in the order the module supplies them, the order its source holds them.
value.2	value	values	Each value MUST be given its zero-based position in the whole input; an input converted in parts, in order, keeps its positions, so that the sheets each part gives, appended sheet by sheet in the file's order, are the whole input's file.
value.3	value	values	Every input value MUST be accepted, of any size, any depth of nesting and any length of string.
value.4	value	values	An input value JSON text rejects, as not a JSON text or as names not unique, MUST be rejected as non-conforming, its reason its zero-based position in the input, a space, and the JSON Pointer to the place within it, in its URI fragment representation: the first object, in the order written, that repeats a name, or else the value itself.
value.5	value	values	What JSON text leaves out MUST be left out of the file, and each place left out reported as not carried by the JSON Pointer to it within the input, whose first reference token is the zero-based position of its input value, as a JSON string.
transfer.1	transfer	transfer	The transfer MUST take the input's tree to relations in normal form, each subordinate relation taking its parent's primary key as a foreign key; where a kind is referred to from several places or holds itself, a foreign key refers to the kind's one relation.
transfer.2	transfer	transfer	A schema's subschemas MUST be taken from its keywords in the order items, additionalItems, properties, patternProperties, additionalProperties, dependencies, if, then, else, allOf, anyOf and oneOf, and within a keyword in the order the schema writes them: the order of the subschemas.
relation.1	relation	relations	A relation is a sheet, and the input values MUST be written as one relation, the sheet of the input values; where a $ref references the root schema, the root schema is a kind, and the sheet of the input values is its kind's one sheet.
relation.2	relation	relations	The schema a $ref references is a kind; every instance that a $ref applies to a child location, or to the same location through anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency, MUST be written to its kind's one sheet, placed by the kind's schema alone, as a child location's instances are, wherever it is referenced.
relation.3	relation	relations	A $ref that allOf applies MUST be read as adding to its instance's own schema, not as a kind.
relation.4	relation	relations	The primitive types an instance location allows MUST be taken as those its type keyword lists, all six where it has none, narrowed by every subschema allOf applies there and by the union of the branches of anyOf and oneOf.
relation.5	relation	relations	By those types, and by whether its schema requires it, each value MUST be written in one of three places: a column of the record that holds it, a simple domain (relation.6, relation.8); a sheet of its own, a nonsimple domain (relation.9-14); or the sheet of instances, molten (relation.15).
relation.6	relation	relations	A property that its schema requires, that is not a kind, and that allows exactly one type, string, number, boolean or null, a simple domain, MUST be written as a column of its sheet, its field holding the property's value.
relation.7	relation	relations	A subordinate sheet whose child instances allow exactly one type, string, number, boolean or null, MUST have one column, _value.
relation.8	relation	relations	A property that its schema requires, that is not a kind, and that allows exactly one type, object, and every subschema allOf applies to a location that allows exactly one type, object, MUST be written as its instance's own: the members such a property holds, and those such a subschema names, written as members of the instance that holds them, and the subschemas the keywords of either apply taken as that instance's.
relation.9	relation	relations	Each subschema applied to child locations that is a property that allows exactly one type, array, or items, each schema of items given as an array, additionalItems, a pattern of patternProperties, or additionalProperties, a repeating group, MUST be struck from its parent and written as a subordinate sheet of its own, whose records are its child instances.
relation.10	relation	relations	A property that allows exactly one type, object, and that its schema does not require, MUST be written as a subordinate sheet, of one record or none.
relation.11	relation	relations	A subschema that anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency applies to the same location, and that allows exactly one type, object, MUST be written as a subordinate sheet of that location's sheet, where the location is not placed branch by branch (relation.12), whose records are the instances whose annotations it collects: under each branch of anyOf and oneOf an instance is valid against, under if where valid, under then or else as if decides, and under a dependency's schema where the instance holds its key.
relation.12	relation	relations	A child location whose anyOf or oneOf gives its branches, and that allows other than exactly one type, or that relation.8 does not write as its instance's own and whose own schema, apart from its branches, gives it no column and no sheet but those the whole file shares, MUST be placed branch by branch: each instance as the location would be if its schema were the first branch, in the order of the subschemas, that the instance is valid against, a property's being taken as not required, the location having no record of its own.
relation.13	relation	relations	A property that allows exactly one type, array, whose items is one schema, MUST be written as the sheet of its elements, and an element that is itself an array as a subordinate sheet of its own; and one whose items is an array of schemas as a sheet of one record per array, with a subordinate sheet for each schema of items and for additionalItems, each holding the elements at its positions.
relation.14	relation	relations	A string that holds FF, a line break or HT MUST be written as its runs of text, as JSON text gives them, to the file's one sheet of runs, which the schema gives where any location can hold a string, each run one record with its page, line and position.
relation.15	relation	relations	Every other value MUST be written molten, to the file's one sheet of instances, which the schema gives where any location is written molten: a property that allows other than exactly one type and is not placed branch by branch (relation.12), or one simple type but is not required; a child location whose subschema allows other than exactly one type and is not placed branch by branch; a member no schema covers (relation.16), and an element at a position no schema of items or additionalItems covers; and the input values themselves, where the root schema allows other than exactly one type, object or array. It holds every instance within the value, the value itself included; where the root schema allows other than exactly one type, object or array, the sheet of the input values is the sheet of instances.
relation.16	relation	relations	Each value MUST be written once. A schema covers a member that its properties names or that a pattern of its patternProperties matches; a member is written by its location's own schema where that covers it; else by the first subschema, in the order of the subschemas, whose annotations the instance collects and that covers it; else by the first additionalProperties that applies, which takes only the members its schema does not cover; else it is written molten (relation.15).
relation.17	relation	relations	A sheet MUST NOT be given a column from an allOf subschema for a property that its location's own schema or an earlier allOf subschema names; where a member is written by an earlier sheet, its field in a later one is empty, its value found by the same key.
relation.19	relation	relations	A sheet MUST NOT be shaped from not, whose annotations are ignored, propertyNames, which applies to names, definitions, which applies to nothing by itself, or contains, whose elements items places.
relation.18	relation	relations	The relation module MUST give each relation the schema gives with the parts of its path, each column with the parts of its label, and each record and cell.
key.1	key	keys	Every record MUST begin with its primary key, its fixed variables first, as void-os.1 requires of every datum.
key.2	key	keys	A record of the sheet of the input values MUST have one key column, _input value, the zero-based position of its input value in the input; where that sheet is the sheet of instances or a kind's sheet, its records are keyed as key.3 keys them, the record of an input value leaving _parent and _pointer empty.
key.3	key	keys	A record of any other sheet but the sheet of runs MUST begin with _input value; then _instance, the zero-based position of its instance among the instances of its input value, the value itself first, counted in the order written; then _parent, the _instance of the record whose instance holds its own, which with _input value is a foreign key, possibly into its own relation; then _pointer, the JSON Pointer to its instance evaluated from that record's instance. A record whose instance is its location's own, a subschema applying to the same location, has the _input value and _instance of its location's record and leaves _parent and _pointer empty. No key holds the access path from the input value to its instance.
key.4	key	keys	A record of the sheet of runs MUST begin with the _input value and _instance of its string, then _page, _line and _position, each a count of the unit FF, LF or HT moves to.
order.1	order	order	Sheets MUST be written in the order of the schema's tree: the sheet of the input values first, each sheet directly followed by its subordinate sheets and theirs in turn, in the order of the subschemas; a property or subschema written as its instance's own gives its sheets in its own place; a kind's sheet, and its subordinate sheets, come in the place of its first reference; and the sheet of instances, where it is not the sheet of the input values, then the sheet of runs, come last.
order.2	order	order	Records MUST be written in the order the input holds them: input values in input order, elements as their array orders them, members and instances as written, an instance before the instances within it, and runs of text in the order of their string.
order.3	order	order	The key columns MUST be written first, then the columns of the simple domains in the order of the schema's tree: a location's own properties in the order the schema writes them, the members of a property written as its instance's own in that property's place, then those an allOf subschema names, in the order of its subschemas.
output.1	output	write	The output MUST write the relations as one MTSV file, at its levels file, sheet, record and field.
output.2	output	sheets	output MUST give the access program sheets of sheet as its own.
output.3	output	write	The file MUST be written by the generator.
file.1	file	file	The whole input MUST be given as the sheets of one MTSV file.
file.2	file	file	A file MUST hold every sheet its schema gives that holds a record, in its place in the order of the schema's tree.
file.3	file	file	A file MUST NOT hold a sheet that holds no record.
sheet.1	sheet	sheets	The sheet of the input values MUST be named by the title of the root schema; where that sheet is the sheet of instances or a kind's sheet, it is named as that sheet is (sheet.2, sheet.3).
sheet.2	sheet	sheets	A kind's sheet MUST be named by the title of its schema, else the last reference token of its $ref.
sheet.3	sheet	sheets	Every sheet name and field name the converter fixes MUST begin with _: the sheet of instances is named _instances, and the sheet of runs _runs; a name taken or composed from the schema's names is written as void-os.4 writes a name taken from outside.
sheet.4	sheet	sheets	Every other sheet MUST be named by its parent sheet's name and the parts of its path within the parent's instance, each after a full stop: the name of each property between them written as its instance's own, and then: for a property, the property's name; for a keyword, the keyword, then the pattern or zero-based position where the keyword holds more than one subschema, and items for the elements of an element that is itself an array; for a subschema that anyOf, oneOf, if, then, else or a dependency applies, the keyword, then the subschema's title, else a dependency's key, else its zero-based position where the keyword holds more than one subschema.
sheet.5	sheet	sheets	Every sheet MUST have a header, its first record, which holds the name of each field, each column labelled with the name of its domain: the key columns by their names, _input value, _instance, _parent, _pointer, _page, _line and _position; a simple domain's column by its property's name; a member of a property written as its instance's own by that property's name, a full stop, and its own name, the role name qualifying the domain name; a member an allOf subschema names by its own name; and _type and _value by those names.
record.1	record	records	Each instance, and each run of text, that a relation holds MUST be one record of its sheet.
field.1	field	fields	Each field MUST hold its instance's text as JSON text gives its _value, null as an empty field.
field.2	field	fields	In the sheet of instances, the fields after a record's keys MUST be _type, its instance's primitive type, and _value, its instance's text, as JSON text gives them.
field.3	field	fields	A string value that holds FF, a line break or HT MUST be written as an empty field, its text written as runs of text to the sheet of runs.
storage.23	storage		The data bank's folder MUST be the absolute path the environment variable the Value data bank variable names holds, where it holds one, and else the user's documents folder joined with the Value data bank folder.
storage.24	storage		The user's documents folder MUST be as PLATFORMDIRS gives it for the platform, from the user's home folder.
storage.25	storage		An input's name MUST be written within the data bank's folder as name within a folder writes it.
storage.17	storage	folder	The access program folder MUST give the data bank's folder, as storage.23 decides it.
storage.1	storage	insert	Storage MUST only insert into an input's relations.
storage.2	storage		Each input MUST be kept as one MTSV file at its name written as storage.25 writes it within the data bank's folder, then .mtsv.
storage.6	storage		One process at a time MUST write: before writing, a lock file named _lock is created in the data bank's folder only where it does not exist, and removed after; a process that finds it waits the Value lock wait and tries again, and takes a lock older than the Value lock timeout as released.
storage.13	storage	insert	The access program insert MUST, holding the lock, convert the values from the position equal to the number of values the input stores, and write the part.
storage.3	storage	insert	A part MUST be written at the end of its input's file, the file opened for appending, its sheets in the file's order, a sheet's name repeating as MTSV permits.
storage.18	storage		A file storage replaces whole MUST be written beside it, at its name then the Value replace suffix, then renamed onto it.
storage.4	storage	insert	Each value MUST be stored with all its records or with none: a value is stored whole once its record of _stored is written, after the part, and the records of a value at or after the number of the input's records of _stored are cut from the input's file before the next part is written.
storage.14	storage	insert	The sheets the input's schema gives MUST be kept, at each insert, as the converter gives them, in the MTSV files _sheets.mtsv and _fields.mtsv in the data bank's folder, each replaced whole, the input's records replacing its earlier ones.
storage.5	storage	mark communicated	The relation _communicated MUST be kept as the MTSV file _communicated.mtsv in the data bank's folder, replaced whole as storage.18 replaces a file.
storage.7	storage	mark communicated	The number of an input's values communicated to a session and agent MUST be kept in _communicated, an input with no record for them having none communicated to them.
storage.8	storage	insert	Each value stored whole MUST have one record in _stored, its _time the time it was stored, in UTC, written as RFC 3339 writes a date and time with the offset Z, and its _event the event handed, the relation kept as the MTSV file _stored.mtsv in the data bank's folder, written at its end, as storage.3 writes a part.
storage.9	storage	insert	The value a source sends MUST be stored as it is sent.
storage.26	storage	insert	A value's time and event MUST be kept only in _stored, beside it.
storage.11	storage	inputs	The access program inputs MUST give the relation _inputs, each input in the order it was first stored, its _values the number of its records of _stored, and its _file its file as storage.2 names it.
storage.15	storage	names	The access program names MUST give _sheets and _fields of every input stored, each input in the order it was first stored.
storage.10	storage	stored	The access program stored MUST give, as the sheet _stored, the records of _stored of the values whose positions lie between first and last, both included.
storage.12	storage		A file system failure, or a file of the data bank the parser rejects, MUST end the access as keeping failed, its reason those words alone, naming no path.
storage.16	storage	insert	An input the converter rejects MUST end the insert as non-conforming, nothing being stored.
storage.19	storage		A file of the data bank that does not exist MUST be read as holding no record, and not as a file system failure.
storage.20	storage	mark communicated	A session or agent holding HT, LF, FF or CR MUST NOT be kept in _communicated, nothing being marked communicated to it.
storage.21	storage	relations	The access program relations MUST give the input's relations from its file, the records of the values at positions below the number of its records of _stored, each sheet name once, its records those of every part in the order appended.
storage.22	storage	communicated	The access program communicated MUST give the number of the input's values communicated to the session and agent, as _communicated keeps it.
communication.1	communication		Communication MUST give what the data bank stores as MTSV, by restriction, and by aggregate only to count an input's values.
communication.5	communication		Communication MUST NOT transform or sort what the data bank stores: values as stored, records in stored order.
communication.2	communication	names	The access program names MUST give three sheets, each record beginning with its primary key: _inputs as storage's inputs gives it; and _sheets and _fields as storage keeps them.
communication.3	communication	records	The access program records MUST give the records of the values whose positions lie between first and last, both included, each counted from 0, of the places asked for, or else every place, each place's sheet named as _sheets names it, in the order of the schema's tree, the records kept by the relational model's kept: those whose first field, _input value, equals one of the positions from first to last, each written as its number is.
communication.4	communication	new	The access program new MUST give the records, as records gives them, of the values from the number communicated to the session and agent asked for to the last value stored, then their records of _stored, then mark every value stored communicated to them.
